        <h2 id="why-china">Why China Dominates Bomber Jacket Manufacturing (And What It Means for Your Brand)</h2>

        <p>China's edge in outerwear comes from cluster density, not from any single factory. Inside a two-hour drive in Zhejiang, Guangdong, or Jiangsu, a sourcing manager can pull shell fabric, rib knit cuffs, YKK-spec zippers, quilted lining, and embroidery service without crossing a provincial border. That proximity compresses sampling cycles and keeps small runs economically viable where fragmented supply chains cannot</p>

        <p>Three practical consequences for a brand launching a bomber line:</p>

        <ul class="ar-list"><li><strong>Sampling speed.</strong> A factory inside a mature cluster converts a tech pack into a fit sample in roughly 7 to 15 days because trims are sourced locally, not imported</li><li><strong>Customization depth.</strong> Rib color, zipper finish, lining print, and embroidery placement stay adjustable through the sampling stage instead of being frozen by a minimum order</li><li><strong>Small-batch feasibility.</strong> For stock shell fabrics, 300 to 500 pieces per color is a workable entry point, enough to test a style before committing capital to volume</li></ul>

        <h2 id="selection-criteria">How We Evaluated These Bomber Jacket Manufacturers (Our Selection Criteria)</h2>

        <p>Five dimensions drive the profiles below. Each maps to a failure mode that has derailed real sourcing programs</p>

        <div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table"><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>What We Look At</th><th>Why It Matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Craft capability</strong></td><td>Rib knit handling, zipper installation, quilting, lining construction</td><td>A factory strong in T-shirts may struggle with structured outerwear</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MOQ flexibility</strong></td><td>Minimum order per color/size, stock vs. custom fabric thresholds</td><td>Determines whether you can test a style before scaling</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Customization depth</strong></td><td>Private label, custom trims, pattern development, wash/finish options</td><td>Separates OEM from true ODM partners</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Compliance readiness</strong></td><td>Documentation availability, audit cooperation, material traceability</td><td>Protects you at customs and with retail buyers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Communication responsiveness</strong></td><td>English capability, sampling feedback speed, issue resolution</td><td>The single biggest predictor of a smooth production run</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

        <p>Weight these to your own situation. A 500-piece streetwear capsule lives or dies on MOQ flexibility and sampling speed. A 20,000-piece retail program for a European chain stands or falls on compliance documentation and sample-to-bulk consistency</p>

        <p>Not sure which factory fits your order profile? <a href="/contact-us/">Get a free sourcing consultation</a> and we'll help you narrow the list</p>

        <h2 id="factory-profiles">10 Bomber Jacket Factory Profiles in China (by Cluster &amp; Specialty, 2026 Review)</h2>

        <p>The ten profiles below span different strengths. Some are built for knit-heavy casual outerwear, others for structured wovens, leather, or technical shells. Match the profile to your product</p>

        <h3>Factory Type 1: Full-Category Outerwear Manufacturer with Deep Customization (YAUUM)</h3>

        <p><strong>Specialty &amp; Craft Strengths</strong></p>

        <p>YAUUM works across six apparel categories: hoodies, T-shirts, jackets, pants, sportswear, and streetwear. For a bomber program that means in-house pattern development and fabric sourcing that crosses category lines, so a brand running jackets and hoodies in the same drop keeps one set of trims, one label spec, and one QC standard. The jacket line covers classic bomber, varsity, coach, and windbreaker silhouettes. Quilting, ribbing, and embroidery run as in-house processes rather than subcontracted add-ons, which is where most lead-time surprises originate</p>

        <p><strong>MOQ, Customization &amp; Lead Time</strong></p>

        <p>MOQ starts around 300 to 500 pieces per color on stock fabrics. Custom-developed shell materials carry higher thresholds because mills set their own dye lot minimums. Sampling runs 7 to 15 days depending on trim availability, bulk production 30 to 45 days, plus 25 to 35 days sea freight to a US or EU port. <a href="/contact-us/">Request a free quote</a> for terms on your specific style</p>

        <p><strong>Best For / Not Ideal For</strong></p>

        <p>A strong fit for brands that want one partner across several categories and for buyers who need pattern development support rather than a finished tech pack. Look elsewhere if you need runs under 100 pieces or genuine leather construction</p>

        <div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table"><thead><tr><th>Positioning</th><th>Customization-focused, multi-category</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Craft strengths</td><td>Ribbing, quilting, embroidery, pattern development</td></tr><tr><td>Cooperation model</td><td>OEM / ODM, private label</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

        <p>Explore <a href="/jackets-manufacturer/">YAUUM custom outerwear services</a> for the full capability breakdown</p>

        <h3>Factory Type 2: Zhejiang Knit Outerwear Specialists</h3>

        <p>Zhejiang's cluster is thick with factories that grew out of knitwear and moved into casual outerwear. They handle soft-shell bombers, jersey-lined styles, and lightweight transitional jackets well, because their machinery and their QC habits were built around fabric hand-feel and rib recovery rather than structure</p>

        <p>Minimums sit around 300 to 600 pieces per style, with sampling in 10 to 20 days and bulk in 35 to 50. Custom rib colors usually trigger a separate dye lot minimum, so ask for that number early</p>

        <p>Casual and streetwear labels tend to get strong value here. Melton structure and leather panels are a different conversation entirely</p>

        <h3>Factory Type 3: Guangdong Woven &amp; Technical Outerwear Factories</h3>

        <p>Guangdong leans woven and technical: water-repellent shells, taped seams, functional linings, mesh venting. If your bomber carries a performance claim on the hangtag, this is the cluster that can substantiate it</p>

        <p>Minimums start at 500 to 1,000 pieces for technical fabrics, sampling takes 15 to 25 days, and bulk runs 40 to 60. Functional fabric mills set their own thresholds, which often becomes your real floor regardless of what the factory quotes</p>

        <p>Choose this cluster when the hangtag makes a performance claim you have to be able to defend. A 300-piece pilot will not get priced here</p>

        <h3>Factory Type 4: Jiangsu Leather &amp; Suede Bomber Makers</h3>

        <p>Jiangsu concentrates leather garment manufacturers with real depth in lambskin, cowhide, and suede. The craft markers here are panel matching across hides, clean edge finishing, and lining attachment that doesn't pull the shell out of shape</p>

        <p>Expect 200 to 500 pieces minimum, sampling 15 to 30 days because hide sourcing and grading take time, and bulk 45 to 70 days. Leather grade drives price and schedule simultaneously; a half-grade change can move both</p>

        <p>The craft justifies the lead time for high-end and heritage brands, provided someone on your team can grade a hide and your target FOB has room in it</p>

        <h3>Factory Type 5: Fujian Sportswear-Oriented Jacket Manufacturers</h3>

        <p>Fujian's sportswear ecosystem turns out athletic bombers, track jackets, and varsity styles, with sublimation, appliqué, and performance trims as everyday work rather than special requests</p>

        <p>Typical terms: 300 to 800 pieces, sampling 10 to 20 days, bulk 35 to 55 days. Team and club programs usually run above those minimums anyway, which is where these factories price best</p>

        <p>Teamwear and sportswear programs sit squarely in this lane; a fashion-led minimal bomber would be an awkward brief</p>

        <p>Running a program across multiple categories? <a href="/contact-us/">Talk to our team</a> about consolidating production under one roof</p>

        <h3>Factory Type 6: Shandong Workwear &amp; Utility Jacket Factories</h3>

        <p>Durability is the organizing principle in Shandong: reinforced seams, heavy-gauge zippers, bartacked stress points, deep functional pocketing. Their bombers read as utility garments, which is either exactly what you want or completely wrong for your line</p>

        <p>Minimums run 500 to 1,000 pieces, sampling 15 to 25 days, bulk 40 to 60. Heavy canvas and duck fabrics slow cutting and sewing, so build that into your calendar</p>

        <p>If your line sheet reads utility, Shandong prices and builds it better than most. A 180g nylon bomber is not their machine</p>

        <h3>Factory Type 7: Guangzhou Streetwear &amp; Graphic Jacket Specialists</h3>

        <p>Guangzhou's streetwear factories are built for decoration: multi-head embroidery, woven and chenille patches, screen print, mixed-media panels. Turnaround speed is the selling point, and trend-driven brands exploit it hard</p>

        <p>Typical terms: 200 to 500 pieces, sampling 7 to 15 days, bulk 30 to 45 days. Decoration complexity, not the shell, drives the sampling clock. A six-color embroidery with 3D puff can add a week on its own</p>

        <p>Speed and decoration depth are the draw for streetwear and DTC brands. The trade-off shows up on repeat orders, where unit cost rarely drops as far as a volume-focused plant can take it</p>

        <h3>Factory Type 8: Jiangxi &amp; Anhui Cost-Efficient Volume Manufacturers</h3>

        <p>Inland provinces trade proximity for labor cost. Basic bombers come out solid; complex detailing frequently goes to subcontractors, which is where visibility drops</p>

        <p>Minimums run 500 to 1,500 pieces, sampling 15 to 25 days, bulk 40 to 60, plus 2 to 4 days of inland trucking to Shanghai or Ningbo port</p>

        <p>The math works for high-volume, cost-sensitive programs that can live with a three-week sampling clock and keep the construction straightforward</p>

        <h3>Factory Type 9: Ningbo Varsity &amp; Collegiate Jacket Makers</h3>

        <p>Ningbo has decades behind it in varsity construction: melton wool bodies, leather or vinyl sleeves, striped rib collars and cuffs, chenille patches. This is a narrow craft, and general outerwear factories rarely execute it cleanly on the first pass</p>

        <p>Expect 300 to 600 pieces, sampling 15 to 25 days, and bulk 45 to 65 days. Chenille patch development runs on its own timeline and often sets the critical path</p>

        <p>Collegiate and heritage programs should start here. It is a specialist's lane, and that narrowness is the whole value</p>

        <h3>Factory Type 10: Shenzhen Tech-Wear &amp; Minimalist Bomber Studios</h3>

        <p>Shenzhen's design-forward workshops handle technical fabrics, bonded and welded seams, hidden closures, and stripped-back silhouettes</p>

        <p>Typical terms: 200 to 500 pieces, sampling 10 to 20 days, bulk 35 to 50 days. Sourcing the fabric usually takes longer than making the garment</p>

        <p>Brands whose positioning rests on construction detail will find their match; budget-driven programs will not recognize the quotes</p>

        <h2 id="craft-essentials">Bomber Jacket Craft Essentials: What Separates a Good Factory from a Great One</h2>

        <p>Four construction details tell you more than any company brochure</p>

        <p><strong>Rib knit cuffs and hem.</strong> Good ribbing returns to shape after stretching. Cheap ribbing goes slack within a few wears and the jacket looks tired on the rack. Ask for rib composition, then test recovery on the physical sample: stretch the cuff to its limit, release, and watch whether it snaps back or sags. Elastane content and knit gauge both matter</p>

        <p><strong>Zipper quality and installation.</strong> A bomber lives or dies by its zipper. Check for smooth pull action under one hand, even stitching along both tapes, and a placket that lies flat without puckering. Metal teeth suit heavier shells and melton wool; coil nylon suits lightweight nylon and polyester styles. Lock the zipper brand and gauge in writing before bulk, because substitution at this stage is common and hard to spot in photos</p>

        <p><strong>Filling and quilting.</strong> Bombers use cotton wadding, synthetic fill, or down. Quilting should be even, with no cold spots and no migration between channels. Hold the sample up to a window: uneven fill shows instantly. For down, ask about fill power and loft recovery after compression, not just weight</p>

        <p><strong>Pattern accuracy.</strong> The classic bomber silhouette depends on a specific shoulder drop and sleeve pitch. A factory that quietly flattens the sleeve cap to make the garment easier to sew will hand you a jacket that fits wrong on a real body. Measure the sample against your spec sheet at chest, cuff, hem, sleeve length, and armhole depth before you approve anything</p>

        <p>Learn more about our <a href="/production/">custom bomber jacket manufacturing process</a> and how these details are controlled in production</p>

        <h2 id="common-pitfalls">Common Pitfalls When Sourcing Bomber Jackets from China (And How to Avoid Them)</h2>

        <p><strong>Sample looks great, bulk doesn't match.</strong> The most common failure mode we see in outerwear sourcing. The defense is procedural: approve a sealed sample that stays at the factory for the duration of the order, and require a pre-production sample from bulk fabric before the cutting table starts</p>

        <p><strong>Hidden MOQ on trims.</strong> The jacket minimum is 300 pieces, then the custom rib turns out to need a 1,000-piece dye lot and your cost model collapses. Ask for MOQ broken out by component: shell fabric, rib, zipper, lining, labels. Never accept a single finished-garment number</p>

        <p><strong>Communication gaps mid-production.</strong> A factory goes quiet for two weeks and then ships something that drifted from the tech pack. Fix the cadence before the deposit clears. One named contact, a weekly update with photos, and a written escalation path</p>

        <p><strong>Missing compliance documentation.</strong> Containers arrive and the paperwork isn't there. During vetting, confirm exactly which documents the factory can issue and ask to see examples from a previous shipment. A vague answer at this stage becomes a customs hold later</p>

        <p><strong>No quality control framework.</strong> When nobody defines inspection points, defects surface in your warehouse instead of theirs. Run a structured <a href="/our-services/">jacket quality control checklist</a> at three gates: pre-production, mid-production, and pre-shipment</p>

        <h2 id="sourcing-checklist">Your Bomber Jacket Sourcing Checklist</h2>

        <p>Work through a written checklist before you contact a single factory. Ours covers:</p>

        <ul class="ar-list"><li>Tech pack completeness and measurement tolerances</li><li>Component-level MOQ questions to ask</li><li>Sample approval and sealing process</li><li>Compliance documentation to request</li><li>Inspection points and acceptance criteria</li><li>Payment terms and shipping Incoterms</li></ul>

        <p><a href="/contact-us/">Talk to our team</a> and score each supplier against the same questions</p>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

          <details class="ar-faq-item" open><summary><h3>What is the typical MOQ for custom bomber jackets from China?</h3></summary><p>For stock fabrics, MOQ commonly falls in the 300 to 500 piece range per color. Custom-developed shell fabrics or special trims can push this to 500 to 1,000 pieces. Always confirm MOQ by component, not just finished garment</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How long does it take to produce a custom bomber jacket order?</h3></summary><p>Sampling typically runs 7 to 25 days depending on fabric and trim sourcing. Bulk production generally takes 30 to 60 days, followed by 25 to 35 days for sea freight. Decoration-heavy styles sit at the long end of the sampling range</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>Can I get a sample before placing a bulk order?</h3></summary><p>Yes. Most manufacturers provide fit samples and pre-production samples at a sample fee that is often credited against the bulk order. Approve and seal a final sample before production begins</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>What materials are commonly used for bomber jackets?</h3></summary><p>Common shells include nylon, polyester, cotton twill, and wool blends. Linings are typically polyester or satin. Filling options range from cotton wadding to synthetic fill to down. Ribbing is usually an elastane-cotton or elastane-polyester blend</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How do I verify a Chinese jacket manufacturer's compliance?</h3></summary><p>Request documentation samples during vetting, ask about audit cooperation, and confirm material traceability for any restricted substances. If a factory cannot produce documents on request, treat that as a red flag</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>What's the price range for custom bomber jackets?</h3></summary><p>FOB pricing for a standard nylon or poly bomber usually lands between $12 and $45 per piece, driven by shell fabric, filling, trim specification, and order volume. Leather and down styles sit well above that band. Get a quote against your own tech pack before building a cost model</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>Do Chinese manufacturers support private label and custom branding?</h3></summary><p>Yes. Private label, custom woven labels, branded zipper pulls, and custom packaging are standard offerings at most established factories. Confirm branding requirements at the sampling stage</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How do I communicate design requirements if I'm not technical?</h3></summary><p>A tech pack with reference images, measurements, and material notes is the standard starting point. If you don't have one, a factory with pattern development capability can build it from your references</p></details>

